<Group labelreference="L862" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Of course, this plot is really comparing the results of two numeric approximations to the true solution of the problem. It is also useful to compare numeric answers to analytic results (if available). For the above choice of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, there is an analytic solution in terms of Airy functions:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L822" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">analytic_sol := dsolve({ode, y(0) = y0}):
analytic_sol := rhs(analytic_sol);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L834" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Here is some code that generates a movie showing how the numerical solution converges to the analytic solution as the number of points is increased.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L885" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">for j from 1 to 25 do:
    N := 4*j:
    p[j] := plot([EulerSol(y0, N), analytic_sol], x = 0..1, title = cat(`number of cells N = `, N), axes = boxed, legend = [`Forward Euler solution`, `Analytic solution`], style = [point, line]):
od:
plots[display](convert(p, list), insequence = true);</Text-field>
</Input>
