<Group labelreference="L841" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Conversely, we could have accomplished the same thing in one line by using the <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">map</Font> command, which applies the same mapping to each element of a list (in this case, we are applying operations to each element of <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">Stencils</Font> to generate a new list <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">stencil</Font>):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L842" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">stencil := 'stencil':
stencil := map(u -&gt; unapply(rhs(u), h, lambda, y[i]), Stencils);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L819" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">It is interesting to note that even though each of the stencils gives a different expression for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, they actually agree with one another to order <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>. To see this, let us perform some Taylor series expansions:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L820" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">for j from 1 to 3 do:
    Labels[j], y[i+1] = series(stencil[j](h, lambda, y[i]), h, 2);
od;</Text-field>
</Input>
